WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.225
Here are five imaginary papers.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.061
Every citation is real within our

00:00:06.081 --> 00:00:09.217
illustration. Every summary is accurate.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.571
Would you trust the answer?

00:00:10.746 --> 00:00:13.445
Now add a sixth paper: the landmark study

00:00:13.465 --> 00:00:15.416
that the answer never mentioned.

00:00:15.591 --> 00:00:17.323
Which part of the system failed?

00:00:19.738 --> 00:00:22.649
A missing citation alone cannot tell us.

00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:25.549
Chapter One asks us to follow the evidence

00:00:25.569 --> 00:00:27.366
before the writing begins.

00:00:28.263 --> 00:00:30.649
Primo Research Assistant provides a

00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:32.130
documented example.

00:00:32.292 --> 00:00:34.309
In the account checked by September twenty

00:00:34.329 --> 00:00:36.847
twenty-six, a language model converts a

00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:39.811
question into Boolean variations connected

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:42.440
by OR. The query searches the Central

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.069
Discovery Index and returns up to thirty

00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:45.996
results.

00:00:46.554 --> 00:00:49.042
Embeddings rerank those candidates to

00:00:49.062 --> 00:00:51.503
identify five sources.

00:00:51.665 --> 00:00:53.694
Those five become the working set.

00:00:53.869 --> 00:00:57.019
Only then does GPT four point one Mini

00:00:57.039 --> 00:00:59.183
write an overview from their abstracts.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:01.697
This is the chapter's dated documentation

00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:04.712
account, not a fresh test of the service.

00:01:05.608 --> 00:01:07.073
Coverage comes first.

00:01:07.248 --> 00:01:09.341
The chapter's October twenty twenty-six

00:01:09.361 --> 00:01:11.867
documentation check says the assistant

00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:15.384
searches CDI metadata and abstracts,

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.756
subject to exclusions including

00:01:17.776 --> 00:01:20.862
insufficient metadata or abstracts and

00:01:20.882 --> 00:01:22.446
some provider collections.

00:01:22.981 --> 00:01:25.170
A record in the library's wider discovery

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.851
system may still fall outside the

00:01:27.871 --> 00:01:29.899
assistant's searchable scope.

00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:33.340
A better query or reranker cannot recover

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.621
a record from a collection the assistant

00:01:35.641 --> 00:01:38.353
cannot search. These nested sets are

00:01:38.373 --> 00:01:40.673
schematic, not measured coverage

00:01:40.693 --> 00:01:41.767
proportions.

00:01:42.658 --> 00:01:44.478
Try the paper-card experiment.

00:01:44.756 --> 00:01:47.171
Thirty cards enter the candidate set.

00:01:47.321 --> 00:01:49.414
Reranking can change their order, and

00:01:49.434 --> 00:01:51.244
selection can keep five.

00:01:51.419 --> 00:01:54.092
Our gold landmark paper never entered

00:01:54.112 --> 00:01:56.566
those thirty. Can a better reranker bring

00:01:56.586 --> 00:01:57.352
it back?

00:01:59.766 --> 00:02:02.208
Reordering a fixed set cannot add another

00:02:02.228 --> 00:02:05.120
member. A new retrieval round could add

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.535
evidence. Fluent writing cannot establish

00:02:08.555 --> 00:02:10.751
source-supported coverage of a paper

00:02:10.771 --> 00:02:12.761
absent from the supplied evidence.

00:02:12.911 --> 00:02:15.352
Retrieval does not delete the original

00:02:15.372 --> 00:02:16.846
records from the index.

00:02:17.748 --> 00:02:20.612
Compare three imaginary cases.

00:02:20.762 --> 00:02:23.151
Paper A is outside the assistant's

00:02:23.171 --> 00:02:24.285
searchable scope.

00:02:24.447 --> 00:02:27.043
Paper B reaches the thirty candidates but

00:02:27.063 --> 00:02:29.633
misses the five-source working set.

00:02:29.795 --> 00:02:32.056
Paper C reaches the writer but is not

00:02:32.076 --> 00:02:32.854
cited.

00:02:33.418 --> 00:02:35.151
A needs coverage evidence.

00:02:35.314 --> 00:02:37.871
B needs candidate and selection traces.

00:02:38.046 --> 00:02:39.972
C needs a comparison between the supplied

00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:41.891
sources and the answer.

00:02:42.053 --> 00:02:45.165
If intermediate evidence is hidden, record

00:02:45.185 --> 00:02:46.582
the uncertainty.

00:02:46.783 --> 00:02:49.121
Finding a paper in a new direct search

00:02:49.141 --> 00:02:52.162
does not reconstruct its earlier path.

00:02:53.065 --> 00:02:55.911
The chapter maps six possible stages:

00:02:55.931 --> 00:02:58.681
query transformation; analysis and

00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:01.735
execution rules; first-stage retrieval;

00:03:01.897 --> 00:03:05.098
fusion; reranking; and presentation.

00:03:05.338 --> 00:03:07.804
Fusion combines candidate lists.

00:03:07.954 --> 00:03:10.292
A controller chooses and sequences the

00:03:10.312 --> 00:03:13.604
actions. Not every product uses every

00:03:13.624 --> 00:03:14.531
stage.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.014
No separate fusion stage is documented in

00:03:18.034 --> 00:03:19.263
the Primo account.

00:03:19.426 --> 00:03:21.777
Elsevier's twenty twenty-four Scopus AI

00:03:21.797 --> 00:03:24.663
account describes query variants, vector

00:03:24.683 --> 00:03:26.956
retrieval and rank fusion.

00:03:27.119 --> 00:03:29.895
That historical example does not establish

00:03:29.915 --> 00:03:32.562
the architecture of every later mode.

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:35.565
Retrieval-augmented generation means

00:03:35.585 --> 00:03:38.374
retrieval selects evidence and a language

00:03:38.394 --> 00:03:40.720
model generates an answer conditioned on

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.310
it. Retrieval limits the supplied

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.390
evidence. It does not guarantee that every

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.168
generated statement comes from that

00:03:48.188 --> 00:03:49.005
evidence.

00:03:49.553 --> 00:03:52.162
A model can draw on its parameters, invent

00:03:52.182 --> 00:03:55.357
a citation or omit a supplied source.

00:03:55.532 --> 00:03:58.540
Before blaming retrieval or generation for

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.860
our missing landmark paper, ask what the

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.702
writer actually received.
